Here is what that means in practice. Each DMR row already carries a limit identity (via limit value and schedule identifiers). We take that linkage as authoritative: we pair the measurement with that specific permit-limit row and do not separately search the permit limit catalog for other rows that might also match on permit, outfall, parameter, and date overlap. On the data we have, every FY2025 measurement (824 of them) ends up with exactly one such pair, so the “one limit per measurement” rule is satisfied on this fixture.

We reached this reading because the DMR extract appears to encode which limit the reporter (or the system) considered applicable at submission time. The measurement rows include limit begin and end dates alongside the monitoring period end date, and we check that the monitoring period falls within that interval — but we do not treat overlapping or staged limit rows in the catalog as competing candidates unless they show up as separate measurement–limit associations. The uniqueness requirement exists because Purpose A asks, for each measurement, whether it exceeds *the* applicable enforceable numeric limit; if more than one limit genuinely governs the same measurement, we would not know which to compare against without further rules.

The distinction matters because getting it wrong would either force a single comparison where several limits actually apply (understating compliance risk), or invent a conflict where the reporting system already resolved which limit governs (creating false ambiguity). It also affects how we handle permit amendments, restated limits, and interval boundaries — cases where the catalog might contain multiple rows that look like the “same” obligation expressed more than once, or where old and new limits overlap during a transition.

One alternative we considered is that applicability should be determined independently from the permit limit catalog: find every limit row matching permit, outfall, parameter, and effective dates, then decide which govern. Under that view, overlapping intervals or staged replacements could yield zero, one, or several applicable limits per measurement, and “exactly one” might be too strict — or might require explicit tie-breaking rules we do not yet have. Another alternative is that multiple applicable limits should be kept as distinct identities (for example, an interim limit and a final limit both in effect during a transition), rather than collapsed to a single governing limit. A third is that catalog restatements of what is effectively the same limit — same numeric value and obligation, different row identity — should not count as multiples even if they appear as separate rows.

We may be wrong about all of this. Please correct anything that does not match how NPDES limit applicability actually works in your experience — including cases we have not thought of. We are not looking for you to choose among the alternatives above; we want your understanding of the real rule, even if it does not fit neatly into any of them.
